"Relates material and information flows across an end-to-end value stream to expose waiting, inventory and production control.\n\nUse when: Improve order-to-delivery flow for one product family. Compare observed current conditions with a deliberately designed future state.\n\nAvoid when: Only a single detailed procedure or reporting hierarchy needs explanation.\n\nVisual grammar: Process boxes show major transformation stages. Distinct symbols separate material movement, inventory and information signals. A timing strip distinguishes processing time from elapsed lead time.\n\nBuild: Select a product family and map backward from its customer. Observe work, queues and control signals at the actual workplace. Record comparable units and dates before proposing a future-state flow.\n\nCheck: All stages belong to the same product family and boundary. Inventory, cycle time and demand use consistent units. Information signals explain how upstream work is authorized.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing the desired process as if it were observed reality. Optimizing isolated stages while increasing total waiting.\n\nExample: A furniture stream shows order signals, cutting, assembly, finishing and shipment, with queues revealing that drying dominates delivery time.\n\nAlternatives: SIPOC diagram, Spaghetti diagram."
